Electromagnetic Capsule Elevator for Next-Generation Skyscrapers: A Certification-Oriented Baseline and Validation Programme

Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract

This paper presents a quantitative, certification-oriented baseline for a human-rated ropeless vertical transportation system intended for high-rise buildings. The proposed architecture uses a double-sided, long-stator, segmented, iron-core permanent-magnet linear synchronous motor with Halbach arrays and a passive permanent-magnet secondary on the capsule. Guidance is provided by mechanical roller guides on continuous steel rails, and power is collected through an enclosed sliding direct-current conductor rail. The design assumes a nominal gross mass of 2000\ \text{kg}, an overload verification mass of 2500\ \text{kg}, a rated electromagnetic force of 25\ \text{kN}, and a nominal upward cruise speed of 10\ \text{m/s}. The paper develops a separated loss model, quantifies normal force cancellation, defines segment switching and power collection requirements, recalculates safe separation, and proposes a certification path based on performance-based safety codes and functional safety standards. A prototype sequence is defined from sliding-contact endurance testing to a full-scale engineering demonstrator.

Keywords: ropeless elevator, linear synchronous motor, vertical transportation, electromagnetic propulsion, functional safety, performance-based certification

---

1. Introduction

High-rise buildings increasingly require vertical transportation systems that overcome the limitations of conventional traction elevators. Rope mass, shaft space, building alignment, and the one-car-per-shaft assumption restrict the scalability of rope-based systems. Ropeless linear-motor elevators have demonstrated the principle of cable-free, multi-cabin operation, while magnetic levitation transport and modern power electronics offer relevant but not directly transferable technologies.

The system proposed here is deliberately conservative for a first human-rated application. Full magnetic levitation and contactless power transfer are excluded from the initial baseline because they increase safety complexity, certification risk, and cost without demonstrably improving first-system reliability. The objective is to establish a credible engineering baseline that can support formal certification and prototype validation.

The present work defines the system architecture, quantifies the dominant mechanical and electromagnetic constraints, develops a loss and power model, specifies safety-critical subsystems, and outlines a certification-oriented test programme.

