3.2 Force model and loss decomposition

The vertical dynamics are described by

m\ddot{x} = F_{em} - mg - F_{loss},

where the loss force is decomposed as

F_{loss} = F_{\text{guide}} + F_{\text{aero}} + F_{\text{pressure}} + F_{\text{eddy}} + F_{\text{pickup}} + F_{\text{misc}}.

Preliminary loss estimates at 10\ \text{m/s} are given in Table 4.

Loss component Main dependency Estimate at 10\ \text{m/s}
Guide roller friction Load, speed 0.5–1.0\ \text{kN}
Aerodynamic drag v^2, capsule shape 0.1–0.3\ \text{kN}
Piston pressure effect Shaft leakage, speed 0.2–0.8\ \text{kN}
Eddy and cogging losses Stator iron, speed 0.5–1.5\ \text{kN}
Power pickup contact Current, contact resistance 0.1–0.4\ \text{kN}
Miscellaneous Seals, cables, HVAC 0.1–0.3\ \text{kN}

The practical cruise loss range is therefore

F_{loss,\text{cruise}} \approx 1.5\text{--}4.3\ \text{kN}.

For preliminary design, a representative cruise loss of 2.5\ \text{kN} is assumed. The wide range indicates that full-scale loss measurement is required before committing to installed power.

3.3 Force sizing

Using the nominal mass and maximum acceleration,

F_{em,0} = m(g+a)
= 2000 \times (9.81 + 1.2)
= 22{,}020\ \text{N}.

Adding estimated acceleration-phase losses gives

F_{em,\text{peak}} \approx 24.0\ \text{kN}.

To provide design margin, the rated electromagnetic force is set at

F_{em,\text{rated}} = 25.0\ \text{kN}.

The constant-speed upward force is

F_{em,\text{cruise}} = mg + F_{loss,\text{cruise}}
\approx 19.62 + 2.5
= 22.12\ \text{kN}.

The resulting mechanical cruise power at 10\ \text{m/s} is

P_{\text{mech}} = F_{em,\text{cruise}} v
= 22.12\ \text{kN} \times 10\ \text{m/s}
= 221.2\ \text{kW}.

Including motor and inverter losses, the electrical power demand is approximately

P_{\text{electrical}} \approx 260\text{--}280\ \text{kW}.

This is the continuous demand for one upward-moving capsule without a counterweight.

