5. Normal Force and Guidance

5.1 Raw normal force

In iron-core permanent-magnet linear synchronous machines, the attractive normal force can be 1.5 to 3 times the thrust:

F_{N,\text{raw}} = \kappa F_{em}, \qquad \kappa \in [1.5,3].

For a 25\ \text{kN} thrust, the raw normal force could therefore reach

F_{N,\text{raw}} \approx 37.5\text{--}75\ \text{kN}.

Such force levels are unacceptable for a lightweight passenger capsule.

5.2 Double-sided cancellation and residual budget

The double-sided stator arrangement uses two opposing stators. The objective is cancellation of the dominant normal forces:

F_{N,\text{left}} + F_{N,\text{right}} \approx 0.

The residual normal force is modelled as

F_{N,\text{res}} = k_g \Delta g + k_i \Delta i + k_m \Delta m,

where \Delta g is the air-gap asymmetry, \Delta i is the current asymmetry, and \Delta m is the magnetisation tolerance. A preliminary worst-case stack is:

· stator current asymmetry \pm 2\%: 1.1\ \text{kN};
· air-gap asymmetry \pm 1\ \text{mm} with stiffness 5\ \text{kN/mm}: 5.0\ \text{kN};
· permanent-magnet tolerance \pm 3\%: 1.1\ \text{kN}.

The worst-case residual is therefore approximately

F_{N,\text{res},\max} \approx 7.2\ \text{kN}.

The mechanical guidance system is consequently rated for a lateral load of 15\ \text{kN}. The nominal residual normal force target is below 4\ \text{kN}, with a worst-case limit of 10\ \text{kN}. This is substantially lower than the uncancelled normal force.

5.3 Guide and alignment

Mechanical roller guides are selected for the first human-rated system. They provide fail-safe lateral constraint independent of electrical power. The nominal electromagnetic air gap is

g = 18\ \text{mm},

and the allowable lateral displacement is

\Delta y_{\text{allow}} = \pm 3\ \text{mm}.

The guide system must maintain this tolerance under building sway, thermal expansion, stator mounting error, and passenger-induced tilt. Four eddy-current gap sensors per side measure the air gap, and a 1\ \text{kHz} gap controller adjusts thrust balance to centre the capsule. The mechanical guides remain the primary safety constraint if active centring is lost.

