6. Distributed Stator and Segment Switching

6.1 Module activation and handover

Only modules near the capsule are fully energised. The switching sequence is:

1. capsule enters the approach zone of the next module;
2. the next module pre-charges and pre-excites to 50\% current;
3. an overlap window of 0.4\ \text{m} begins;
4. thrust ramps from the outgoing to the incoming module over 20–40\ \text{ms};
5. the outgoing module de-excites and enters standby.

At 10\ \text{m/s}, the time to cross one 2\ \text{m} module is

t_{\text{module}} = \frac{L_{\text{module}}}{v}
= \frac{2.0}{10}
= 0.2\ \text{s}.

The overlap time is

t_{\text{overlap}} = \frac{L_{\text{overlap}}}{v}
= \frac{0.4}{10}
= 0.04\ \text{s}.

During overlap, thrust sharing is feedforward-controlled:

F_{\text{out}} = F_{\text{ref}} \left(1 - \frac{s}{L_{\text{overlap}}}\right),

F_{\text{in}} = F_{\text{ref}} \frac{s}{L_{\text{overlap}}},

where s is the position within the overlap region. To achieve a force dip below 2\%, the current control loop must operate at or above 10\ \text{kHz}, the position loop at or above 1\ \text{kHz}, and the field-programmable gate array logic must have a command latency below 50\ \mu\text{s}.

6.2 Fault response and N-1 isolation

If one module fails under load:

· adjacent modules increase current;
· acceleration is reduced to 0.6\ \text{m/s}^2;
· the capsule proceeds to the nearest landing;
· the safety controller monitors thrust imbalance;
· the mechanical brake remains ready.

The system is designed so that the loss of one active module does not produce a passenger-perceptible jerk. The distributed modular architecture enables fault isolation: a failed module can be bypassed, and traffic rerouted to avoid the faulty zone where shaft geometry permits.

