7.2 Arc and wear management

At 800\ \text{V} DC and 500\ \text{A}, the arc energy is substantial. The main risks and mitigations are:

· Contact bounce arcing: controlled contact force, shoe damping, and bounce time below 1\ \text{ms}.
· Arc-flash hazard: enclosed busbar, arc-containment barriers tested to IEC 61641, and 50\ \text{m} busbar segmentation with fast DC breakers.
· Moisture and dust: sealed shaft sections and IP54 enclosures.
· Contact wear: wear sensors, scheduled replacement, and voltage-drop monitoring.
· Fire: fire-rated enclosure, smoke detection, and shaft fire suppression.

Because sliding power collection at 10\ \text{m/s} and 350\ \text{A} is one of the highest-risk subsystems, it is assigned a dedicated prototype phase before the motor module test.

