8. Braking and Safe Separation

8.1 Triple braking architecture

The emergency braking system comprises three independent layers:

1. Regenerative braking using the propulsion motor in generator mode;
2. Independent normally-closed eddy-current retarder;
3. Spring-applied mechanical safety gear engaging the guide rails.

The mechanical safety gear is the certified ultimate safety layer. Its minimum average deceleration is

a_{\text{brake}} = 0.6g = 5.886\ \text{m/s}^2.

For an initial speed of 10\ \text{m/s}, the braking distance is

d_{\text{brake}} = \frac{v^2}{2a_{\text{brake}}}
= \frac{10^2}{2 \times 5.886}
\approx 8.5\ \text{m}.

A preliminary reaction time of

t_{\text{react}} = 0.3\ \text{s}

adds

d_{\text{react}} = v t_{\text{react}}
= 10 \times 0.3
= 3.0\ \text{m}.

The overspeed governor acts directly on the mechanical brake, independent of software.

8.2 Safe separation distance

The safe separation is calculated from

d_{\text{safe}} = S_f \left( d_{\text{brake}} + d_{\text{react}} + d_{\text{sensor}} + d_{\text{control}} + d_{\text{thermal}} \right),

where the contributions are:

· certified braking distance: 8.5\ \text{m};
· reaction distance: 3.0\ \text{m};
· sensor uncertainty: 1.0\ \text{m};
· control margin, including one capsule accelerating while another brakes: 5.0\ \text{m};
· vibration and thermal position noise: 2.5\ \text{m}.

The sum is 20.0\ \text{m}. Applying a safety factor S_f = 1.5 gives

d_{\text{safe}} = 30.0\ \text{m}.

This separation must be confirmed by full-scale braking tests. A shorter separation is rejected as unsafe because it would not accommodate sensor uncertainty, building vibration, and worst-case relative motion.

