9. Energy Architecture and Storage

9.1 Potential energy and regenerative recovery

For one 2000\ \text{kg} capsule descending 500\ \text{m}, the potential energy is

E_p = mgh
= 2000 \times 9.81 \times 500
= 9.81\ \text{MJ}
= 2.725\ \text{kWh}.

With generator and converter efficiency \eta = 85\%, the recoverable energy is

E_{\text{recoverable}} = \eta E_p
= 0.85 \times 2.725
= 2.32\ \text{kWh}.

The design targets are:

· regenerative capture \ge 65\%;
· cabin-to-cabin energy reuse \ge 30\% during simultaneous traffic;
· grid peak reduction \ge 40\% during morning up-peak;
· standby module power <10\ \text{W} per module.

9.2 Supercapacitor and battery sizing

A supercapacitor bank of 800\ \text{V} and 10\ \text{F} stores

E_{\text{sc}} = \frac{1}{2} C V^2
= \frac{1}{2} \times 10 \times 800^2
= 3.2\ \text{MJ}
= 0.89\ \text{kWh}.

The peak regenerative power for one braking capsule is approximately

P_{\text{regen}} = F_{\text{brake}} v
\approx 25\ \text{kN} \times 10\ \text{m/s}
= 250\ \text{kW}.

A 2\ \text{s} braking event requires about 0.14\ \text{kWh}, so the 0.89\ \text{kWh} supercapacitor bank can absorb several successive braking transients.

A lithium-titanate-oxide battery of 48\ \text{V}, 50\ \text{Ah} provides

E_{\text{LTO}} = 2.4\ \text{kWh}.

This battery is intended for emergency lighting, ventilation, communication, and door locking, not for normal propulsion.

9.3 Emergency energy budget

The worst-case emergency movement assumes the capsule must travel 25\ \text{m} to the nearest landing:

E_{\text{move}} = mg\Delta z
= 2000 \times 9.81 \times 25
= 490.5\ \text{kJ}
= 0.136\ \text{kWh}.

Thirty minutes of emergency lighting, ventilation, and communication at 300\ \text{W} require

E_{\text{aux}} = 0.3\ \text{kW} \times 0.5\ \text{h}
= 0.15\ \text{kWh}.

The total emergency energy requirement is therefore approximately

E_{\text{emergency}} \approx 0.286\ \text{kWh},

which is well below the 2.4\ \text{kWh} LTO battery capacity.

