10. Pressure, Fire, and Building Integration

10.1 Pressure management

For a 500\ \text{m} shaft, the external pressure change is

\Delta p_{\text{ext}} = \rho_{\text{air}} g H
\approx 1.2 \times 9.81 \times 500
= 5886\ \text{Pa}.

At 10\ \text{m/s}, the external pressure rate is

\dot{p}_{\text{ext}} = \rho_{\text{air}} g v
= 1.2 \times 9.81 \times 10
\approx 117.7\ \text{Pa/s}.

To limit passenger ear-pressure change to 2000\ \text{Pa}, the cabin pressure controller must maintain a total cabin pressure change below 2000\ \text{Pa} and an instantaneous rate below 500\ \text{Pa/s}. Over a 50\ \text{s} ascent, the average cabin pressure rate is therefore

\dot{p}_{\text{cab,avg}} \le \frac{2000}{50}
= 40\ \text{Pa/s}.

This is achieved with a sealed or actively compensated cabin, aerodynamic nose and tail shaping, shaft pressure relief paths, and controlled door-seal leakage.

10.2 Thermal expansion and building sway

Steel guide rails have a thermal expansion coefficient \alpha_{\text{steel}} = 12 \times 10^{-6}\ \text{K}^{-1}. For a 50\ \text{m} segment and a 40\ \text{K} temperature rise,

\Delta L_{\text{steel}} = \alpha_{\text{steel}} L \Delta T
= 12 \times 10^{-6} \times 50 \times 40
= 24\ \text{mm}.

Copper busbars have \alpha_{\text{copper}} = 17 \times 10^{-6}\ \text{K}^{-1}, giving

\Delta L_{\text{copper}} = 17 \times 10^{-6} \times 50 \times 40
= 34\ \text{mm}.

Expansion joints are therefore required every 50\ \text{m}, with sliding stator mounts and thermal compensation in the control model.

Tall-building lateral drift can reach \pm 200\ \text{mm} at a 1000\ \text{m} height. The guide system is designed for \pm 10\ \text{mm} lateral compliance, and the 18\ \text{mm} electromagnetic air gap provides additional margin beyond the \pm 3\ \text{mm} control target.

10.3 Fire and smoke strategy

The normal pressure-relief strategy must be integrated with fire-mode shaft pressurisation. The proposed approach uses zoned shaft pressurisation:

· in normal operation, pressure relief paths prevent excessive piston pressure;
· in fire mode, shaft pressurisation prevents smoke ingress;
· smoke control zones are separated by fire-rated doors and dampers;
· the capsule stops at the nearest non-fire floor;
· doors remain closed unless the landing is safe;
· fire-rated materials provide at least 30\ \text{min} protection.

