11. Safety and Certification

11.1 Safety layers

The governing principle is that loss of electromagnetic propulsion must never result in uncontrolled capsule motion. The safety layers are:

1. normal electromagnetic propulsion control;
2. regenerative braking;
3. independent normally-closed eddy-current retarder;
4. mechanical wedge safety gear on guide rails;
5. overspeed governor;
6. redundant SIL 3 position and velocity sensing;
7. emergency local power;
8. fire and smoke containment;
9. rescue and evacuation.

11.2 Control hierarchy

The control architecture is layered:

· Safety controller: independent hardware, hard separation constraints, direct brake command authority, cannot be overridden.
· Certified real-time controller: motor current and position loops, 10\ \text{kHz} current loop and 1\ \text{kHz} position loop.
· Motion planner: jerk-limited trajectories, energy-optimal coasting where possible.
· Traffic manager: deterministic multi-capsule scheduling, destination grouping, hard separation enforcement.
· Advisory optimisation layer: artificial intelligence and digital twin for predictive maintenance, traffic prediction, and energy optimisation.

The advisory layer is explicitly outside the safety chain. It may suggest schedules and modify objective functions, but it must not command thrust, brake release, or door operation. The safety controller is the only layer that can directly command brakes and thrust limits.

11.3 Certification path

Conventional prescriptive codes such as EN 81-20 and ASME A17.1 assume ropes, counterweights, and one car per shaft. A ropeless multi-capsule system therefore requires a performance-based safety case.

The proposed certification path uses ASME A17.7 / CSA B44.7 as the primary framework, with the following steps:

1. risk assessment according to ISO 14798;
2. mapping of Global Essential Safety Requirements;
3. design verification through analysis, simulation, and test;
4. independent review by an Accredited Elevator/Escalator Certification Organization;
5. functional safety assessment according to IEC 61508 for the required safety integrity level;
6. safety component testing under EN 81-50 where applicable, with risk-assessed deviations for rope-based components.

The target safety integrity levels are:

· SIL 3 for position sensing, velocity sensing, overspeed protection, and emergency braking;
· SIL 2 for propulsion control only when the independent safety layer remains active.

