13. Conclusion

The proposed system is a cable-free, counterweight-free capsule using a double-sided, long-stator, segmented, iron-core permanent-magnet linear synchronous motor with Halbach arrays. It employs mechanical roller guidance, enclosed sliding DC power collection, a regenerative 800\ \text{V} DC distribution system, and independent mechanical emergency braking.

The baseline establishes a nominal mass of 2000\ \text{kg}, an overload verification mass of 2500\ \text{kg}, a rated electromagnetic force of 25\ \text{kN}, and a safe separation distance of 30\ \text{m}. The loss model is separated into guide, aerodynamic, pressure, eddy-current, pickup, and miscellaneous contributions. Normal force is quantified and mitigated by double-sided stator cancellation, with the mechanical guide system rated for the residual lateral load. Segment switching is specified at the module, timing, and fault-response level. Power collection is treated as a safety-critical subsystem. Pressure management, thermal expansion, building sway, fire, and rescue are addressed as integral design constraints.

The proposed certification path is performance-based, following ASME A17.7 and IEC 61508, with early engagement of an accredited certification organisation. The next step is not full-scale building installation but a focused prototype campaign beginning with sliding-contact endurance, single-module thrust validation, and two-module handover. Only after these tests are completed should the architecture be revised and the certification evidence package expanded.

