Appendix B. Loss Model Derivations and Uncertainty Ranges

The total loss force is decomposed as

F_{loss} = F_{\text{guide}} + F_{\text{aero}} + F_{\text{pressure}} + F_{\text{eddy}} + F_{\text{pickup}} + F_{\text{misc}}.

Each component is modelled as follows.

B.1 Guide roller friction

The guide rollers support the residual normal force F_{N,\text{res}} and lateral loads. The friction force is

F_{\text{guide}} = \mu_r F_{N,\text{res}} + \mu_r F_{\text{lateral}},

where \mu_r \approx 0.02–0.05 for rolling-element guides. With F_{N,\text{res}} \approx 4–10\ \text{kN} and lateral load 2\ \text{kN},

F_{\text{guide}} \approx 0.02 \times 6000 + 0.05 \times 12000,

which lies in the range 0.12–0.60\ \text{kN}. The conservative estimate is 0.5–1.0\ \text{kN} to account for rail misalignment and wear.

B.2 Aerodynamic drag

The aerodynamic drag in a semi-sealed shaft is

F_{\text{aero}} = \frac{1}{2} \rho_{\text{air}} C_d A_{\text{eff}} v^2,

where \rho_{\text{air}} = 1.2\ \text{kg/m}^3, C_d \in [0.3,0.8], A_{\text{eff}} \approx 1.8\ \text{m}^2, and v = 10\ \text{m/s}. Therefore,

F_{\text{aero}} = 0.5 \times 1.2 \times 0.5 \times 1.8 \times 100
\approx 54\ \text{N}

for C_d = 0.5. With C_d = 0.8,

F_{\text{aero}} \approx 86\ \text{N}.

These are significantly lower than the preliminary 0.1–0.3\ \text{kN} because the effective frontal area is small. The upper bound accounts for piston-effect amplification in narrow shaft gaps.

B.3 Piston pressure effect

The capsule acts as a piston in the shaft. The pressure difference across the capsule is

\Delta p_{\text{piston}} = \frac{1}{2} \rho_{\text{air}} v^2 \left( \frac{1}{\beta^2} - 1 \right),

where \beta is the effective bypass ratio. For a shaft with capsule area A_{\text{cap}} and shaft area A_{\text{shaft}},

\beta = \frac{A_{\text{shaft}} - A_{\text{cap}}}{A_{\text{shaft}}}.

With a 3.5\ \text{m} \times 3.5\ \text{m} shaft and 1.6\ \text{m} \times 1.8\ \text{m} capsule,

A_{\text{shaft}} = 12.25\ \text{m}^2, \quad A_{\text{cap}} = 2.88\ \text{m}^2,

\beta = \frac{12.25 - 2.88}{12.25} = 0.765.

The pressure-induced force is

F_{\text{pressure}} = \Delta p_{\text{piston}} A_{\text{cap}}.

For v = 10\ \text{m/s},

\Delta p_{\text{piston}} = 0.5 \times 1.2 \times 100 \left( \frac{1}{0.765^2} - 1 \right)
= 60 \times (1.708 - 1)
= 42.5\ \text{Pa}.

Thus,

F_{\text{pressure}} = 42.5 \times 2.88
\approx 122\ \text{N}.

The preliminary range 0.2–0.8\ \text{kN} includes additional effects such as unsteady flow, shaft leakage variation, and door seal leakage.

B.4 Eddy-current and cogging losses

Eddy-current losses in the iron core scale approximately linearly with speed:

F_{\text{eddy}} \approx \frac{P_{\text{eddy}}}{v} = \frac{k_e v}{v} = k_e.

Cogging force is speed-independent but position-dependent. A preliminary estimate gives 0.5–1.5\ \text{kN} total. This must be measured in P1.

B.5 Power pickup contact loss

The contact resistance of a sliding shoe is approximately R_c \approx 1\ \text{m}\Omega. At 350\ \text{A},

P_{\text{contact}} = I^2 R_c = 350^2 \times 0.001 = 122.5\ \text{W}.

The equivalent force is

F_{\text{pickup}} = \frac{P_{\text{contact}}}{v}
= \frac{122.5}{10}
= 12.25\ \text{N}.

This is negligible. The preliminary 0.1–0.4\ \text{kN} includes mechanical friction of the shoes, arc drag, and busbar interface losses.

B.6 Miscellaneous losses

Seals, flexible cables, HVAC, and other parasitic loads contribute 0.1–0.3\ \text{kN}.

B.7 Total loss range

Summing the central estimates:

F_{loss,\text{cruise}} \approx 0.75 + 0.15 + 0.25 + 1.0 + 0.2 + 0.2
= 2.55\ \text{kN}.

The total practical range remains

F_{loss,\text{cruise}} \in [1.5, 4.3]\ \text{kN}.

For design, the representative value 2.5\ \text{kN} is used.

