Appendix C. Electromagnetic Force and Module Thrust Contribution Proof

C.1 Required thrust

The required electromagnetic force at peak acceleration is

F_{em,\text{peak}} = m_{\text{gross}}(g + a_{\max}) + F_{loss,\text{accel}}.

Using m_{\text{gross}} = 2000\ \text{kg}, g = 9.81\ \text{m/s}^2, a_{\max} = 1.2\ \text{m/s}^2,

F_{em,0} = 2000 \times (9.81 + 1.2)
= 2000 \times 11.01
= 22{,}020\ \text{N}.

Adding F_{loss,\text{accel}} \approx 2.0\ \text{kN},

F_{em,\text{peak}} \approx 24.0\ \text{kN}.

The rated force is selected as

F_{em,\text{rated}} = 25.0\ \text{kN}.

At cruise,

F_{em,\text{cruise}} = mg + F_{loss,\text{cruise}}
= 2000 \times 9.81 + 2500
= 19{,}620 + 2500
= 22{,}120\ \text{N}
= 22.12\ \text{kN}.

C.2 Module thrust contribution

The module parameters are:

· module length L_{\text{mod}} = 2.0\ \text{m};
· nominal continuous thrust per module F_{\text{mod,nom}} = 6.5\ \text{kN};
· peak overload thrust per module F_{\text{mod,peak}} = 8.5\ \text{kN} for 10\ \text{s};
· active modules around capsule N_{\text{active}} = 4;
· adjacent pre-excited modules N_{\text{adj}} = 1.

The available continuous thrust is

F_{\text{avail,cont}} = N_{\text{active}} \times F_{\text{mod,nom}}
= 4 \times 6.5
= 26.0\ \text{kN}.

This exceeds the rated 25.0\ \text{kN}, giving a margin of 1.0\ \text{kN}. The available peak thrust is

F_{\text{avail,peak}} = N_{\text{active}} \times F_{\text{mod,peak}}
= 4 \times 8.5
= 34.0\ \text{kN}.

This exceeds the peak requirement of 24.0\ \text{kN} by a factor of 1.42.

C.3 N-1 fault tolerance

If one of the four active modules fails, the remaining continuous thrust is

F_{\text{remain}} = 3 \times 6.5 = 19.5\ \text{kN}.

At cruise, the required thrust is 22.12\ \text{kN}, so the remaining thrust is insufficient for constant speed but sufficient for controlled deceleration. The available deceleration is

a_{\text{decel}} = \frac{mg - F_{\text{remain}}}{m}
= \frac{19{,}620 - 19{,}500}{2000}
= 0.06\ \text{m/s}^2.

This is a very gentle deceleration; however, with the eddy-current retarder and mechanical brake available, the system can still stop safely. The system is designed to reduce speed and proceed to the nearest landing under reduced acceleration. Additionally, adjacent modules can be temporarily overloaded to 8.5\ \text{kN} for short durations, giving

F_{\text{emergency}} = 3 \times 8.5 = 25.5\ \text{kN},

which is sufficient to maintain near-nominal thrust for a limited time.

