Appendix E. Segment Switching Timing and Force Dip Analysis

E.1 Module transit time

At v = 10\ \text{m/s} and L_{\text{mod}} = 2.0\ \text{m},

t_{\text{module}} = \frac{L_{\text{mod}}}{v} = \frac{2.0}{10} = 0.2\ \text{s}.

E.2 Overlap window

The overlap distance is L_{\text{overlap}} = 0.4\ \text{m}. The overlap time is

t_{\text{overlap}} = \frac{0.4}{10} = 0.04\ \text{s} = 40\ \text{ms}.

E.3 Force sharing

During overlap, the outgoing module force decreases linearly, and the incoming module force increases linearly:

F_{\text{out}}(s) = F_{\text{ref}} \left(1 - \frac{s}{L_{\text{overlap}}}\right),

F_{\text{in}}(s) = F_{\text{ref}} \frac{s}{L_{\text{overlap}}},

where s \in [0, L_{\text{overlap}}] is the position within the overlap region.

The total force during overlap is

F_{\text{total}}(s) = F_{\text{out}}(s) + F_{\text{in}}(s)
= F_{\text{ref}} \left(1 - \frac{s}{L_{\text{overlap}}} + \frac{s}{L_{\text{overlap}}}\right)
= F_{\text{ref}}.

This proves that ideal linear sharing produces zero force dip. In practice, force dip arises from current loop bandwidth, latency, and saturation. The control requirements are:

· current loop bandwidth \ge 10\ \text{kHz};
· position loop \ge 1\ \text{kHz};
· FPGA command latency <50\ \mu\text{s}.

The expected force dip is <2\% of F_{\text{ref}}.

E.4 Force dip due to timing error

Assume a timing mismatch \Delta t between outgoing and incoming current ramps. The force error is

\Delta F \approx F_{\text{ref}} \frac{\Delta t}{t_{\text{overlap}}}.

For \Delta t = 0.2\ \text{ms} and t_{\text{overlap}} = 40\ \text{ms},

\Delta F \approx 25 \times \frac{0.2}{40}
= 0.125\ \text{kN}.

As a fraction,

\frac{\Delta F}{F_{\text{ref}}} = \frac{0.125}{25} = 0.005 = 0.5\%.

This is within the 2\% target.

