Appendix F. Braking Dynamics and Safe Separation Proof

F.1 Certified braking distance

The mechanical safety gear must achieve a minimum average deceleration of

a_{\text{brake}} = 0.6g = 0.6 \times 9.81 = 5.886\ \text{m/s}^2.

For an initial speed v_0 = 10\ \text{m/s}, the braking distance is

d_{\text{brake}} = \frac{v_0^2}{2a_{\text{brake}}}
= \frac{10^2}{2 \times 5.886}
= \frac{100}{11.772}
\approx 8.5\ \text{m}.

F.2 Reaction distance

The reaction time includes sensor detection, logic processing, and mechanical actuation:

t_{\text{react}} = t_{\text{sensor}} + t_{\text{logic}} + t_{\text{actuation}}
\approx 0.3\ \text{s}.

The reaction distance at 10\ \text{m/s} is

d_{\text{react}} = v_0 t_{\text{react}}
= 10 \times 0.3
= 3.0\ \text{m}.

F.3 Safe separation

The safe separation is

d_{\text{safe}} = S_f \left( d_{\text{brake}} + d_{\text{react}} + d_{\text{sensor}} + d_{\text{control}} + d_{\text{thermal}} \right),

where the additional margins are:

· sensor uncertainty: 1.0\ \text{m};
· control margin, including one capsule accelerating while another brakes: 5.0\ \text{m};
· vibration and thermal position noise: 2.5\ \text{m}.

The base sum is

d_{\text{base}} = 8.5 + 3.0 + 1.0 + 5.0 + 2.5 = 20.0\ \text{m}.

Applying a safety factor S_f = 1.5,

d_{\text{safe}} = 1.5 \times 20.0 = 30.0\ \text{m}.

This is the minimum safe separation at 10\ \text{m/s}. It must be confirmed by full-scale braking tests.

