Appendix G. Energy Storage Sizing and Emergency Energy Budget

G.1 Potential energy and regenerative recovery

For a 2000\ \text{kg} capsule descending 500\ \text{m},

E_p = mgh = 2000 \times 9.81 \times 500
= 9.81 \times 10^6\ \text{J}
= 2.725\ \text{kWh}.

With an overall generator and converter efficiency \eta = 0.85, the recoverable energy is

E_{\text{recoverable}} = \eta E_p
= 0.85 \times 2.725
= 2.316\ \text{kWh}.

The design target is regenerative capture \ge 65\%, corresponding to

E_{\text{target}} \ge 0.65 \times 2.725 = 1.771\ \text{kWh}.

G.2 Supercapacitor bank sizing

A supercapacitor bank with C = 10\ \text{F} at V = 800\ \text{V} stores

E_{\text{sc}} = \frac{1}{2} C V^2
= 0.5 \times 10 \times 800^2
= 3.2 \times 10^6\ \text{J}
= 0.889\ \text{kWh}.

The peak regenerative power for one braking capsule is

P_{\text{regen}} = F_{\text{brake}} v
\approx 25{,}000 \times 10
= 250\ \text{kW}.

A 2\ \text{s} braking event requires

E_{\text{braking}} = P_{\text{regen}} \Delta t
= 250{,}000 \times 2
= 500{,}000\ \text{J}
= 0.139\ \text{kWh}.

Thus, the supercapacitor bank can absorb

n = \frac{E_{\text{sc}}}{E_{\text{braking}}}
= \frac{0.889}{0.139}
\approx 6.4

successive braking events without recharging.

G.3 LTO battery sizing

A lithium-titanate-oxide battery with V = 48\ \text{V} and Q = 50\ \text{Ah} stores

E_{\text{LTO}} = V Q = 48 \times 50
= 2400\ \text{Wh}
= 2.4\ \text{kWh}.

G.4 Emergency energy budget

Worst-case emergency movement assumes the capsule must travel 25\ \text{m} to the nearest landing:

E_{\text{move}} = m g \Delta z
= 2000 \times 9.81 \times 25
= 490{,}500\ \text{J}
= 0.136\ \text{kWh}.

Thirty minutes of emergency lighting, ventilation, and communication at 300\ \text{W} requires

E_{\text{aux}} = P_{\text{aux}} t
= 0.3\ \text{kW} \times 0.5\ \text{h}
= 0.15\ \text{kWh}.

Total emergency energy requirement:

E_{\text{emergency}} = 0.136 + 0.15 = 0.286\ \text{kWh}.

The LTO battery capacity of 2.4\ \text{kWh} provides a factor of

\frac{2.4}{0.286} \approx 8.4

margin over the worst-case requirement.

