Appendix H. Pressure Management Calculations

H.1 External pressure change

For a 500\ \text{m} shaft, the external pressure change is

\Delta p_{\text{ext}} = \rho_{\text{air}} g H.

Using \rho_{\text{air}} = 1.2\ \text{kg/m}^3,

\Delta p_{\text{ext}} = 1.2 \times 9.81 \times 500
= 5886\ \text{Pa}.

H.2 External pressure rate

At 10\ \text{m/s},

\dot{p}_{\text{ext}} = \rho_{\text{air}} g v
= 1.2 \times 9.81 \times 10
= 117.7\ \text{Pa/s}.

H.3 Cabin pressure control requirement

To limit passenger ear-pressure change to \Delta p_{\text{ear,max}} = 2000\ \text{Pa} over the full ascent, the average cabin pressure rate must satisfy

\dot{p}_{\text{cab,avg}} \le \frac{\Delta p_{\text{ear,max}}}{t_{\text{ascent}}}.

For a 500\ \text{m} ascent at 10\ \text{m/s} with acceleration and deceleration phases, the approximate ascent time is 50\ \text{s} plus acceleration time; approximately 55\ \text{s}. Conservatively,

\dot{p}_{\text{cab,avg}} \le \frac{2000}{50} = 40\ \text{Pa/s}.

The instantaneous cabin pressure rate must not exceed

\dot{p}_{\text{cab,max}} = 500\ \text{Pa/s}.

Thus, the cabin pressure control system must maintain

\dot{p}_{\text{cab}} \le 500\ \text{Pa/s}

and total change below 2000\ \text{Pa}.

