Appendix I. Thermal Expansion and Building Sway Calculations

I.1 Thermal expansion of steel guide rails

The thermal expansion is

\Delta L = \alpha L \Delta T.

For steel, \alpha_{\text{steel}} = 12 \times 10^{-6}\ \text{K}^{-1}, L = 50\ \text{m}, \Delta T = 40\ \text{K},

\Delta L_{\text{steel}} = 12 \times 10^{-6} \times 50 \times 40
= 0.024\ \text{m}
= 24\ \text{mm}.

I.2 Thermal expansion of copper busbars

For copper, \alpha_{\text{copper}} = 17 \times 10^{-6}\ \text{K}^{-1},

\Delta L_{\text{copper}} = 17 \times 10^{-6} \times 50 \times 40
= 0.034\ \text{m}
= 34\ \text{mm}.

Expansion joints are required every 50\ \text{m} to accommodate these displacements.

I.3 Building sway

Tall-building lateral drift can reach 0.5\% of height. For a 1000\ \text{m} building,

\Delta y_{\text{drift}} = 0.005 \times 1000 = 5.0\ \text{m}.

However, this is the top-floor drift relative to the base. The guide system is designed for local lateral compliance of \pm 10\ \text{mm}. The electromagnetic air gap is 18\ \text{mm}, and the control target is \pm 3\ \text{mm}. Therefore, the guide system has 10\ \text{mm} compliance and the air gap provides an additional 7\ \text{mm} clearance beyond the control target.

