Appendix M. Control Barrier Function for Collision Avoidance

M.1 Problem statement

For two capsules i and j in the same shaft, define the relative position

z = x_i - x_j.

The hard safety constraint is

|z| > d_{\text{safe}}.

Assume capsule i is above capsule j, so z > 0. The constraint becomes

z \ge d_{\text{safe}}.

M.2 Control barrier function

Define the barrier function

h(z) = z - d_{\text{safe}}.

The safety condition is h(z) \ge 0. The time derivative is

\dot{h} = \dot{z} = v_i - v_j.

A control barrier function enforces

\dot{h} + \alpha h \ge 0,

where \alpha > 0 is a design parameter. This ensures that if h \ge 0, it remains non-negative.

M.3 Enforcement

The safety controller enforces the CBF condition at 100\ \text{Hz}:

v_i - v_j + \alpha (x_i - x_j - d_{\text{safe}}) \ge 0.

If the condition is violated, the controller commands braking. This provides a formal guarantee of collision avoidance independent of the traffic manager.

