Appendix N. Power Collection Arc Flash Energy Estimate

The arc flash energy in a DC system can be estimated using the maximum power method:

E_{\text{arc}} = P_{\text{arc}} t_{\text{arc}},

where

P_{\text{arc}} \approx \frac{V_{\text{dc}} I_{\text{arc}}}{2}.

For V_{\text{dc}} = 800\ \text{V} and a prospective fault current I_{\text{arc}} = 500\ \text{A},

P_{\text{arc}} = \frac{800 \times 500}{2} = 200{,}000\ \text{W} = 200\ \text{kW}.

If the DC breaker clears the fault in 20\ \text{ms},

E_{\text{arc}} = 200{,}000 \times 0.02 = 4{,}000\ \text{J}.

The enclosure must contain this energy without rupture. The busbar segmentation into 50\ \text{m} sections limits the available fault current and arc duration.

