Appendix O. N-1 Fault Tolerance Proof

The system has N_{\text{active}} = 4 modules active. The nominal continuous thrust per module is F_{\text{mod,nom}} = 6.5\ \text{kN}.

O.1 Normal operation

Total continuous thrust:

F_{\text{total,cont}} = N_{\text{active}} F_{\text{mod,nom}}
= 4 \times 6.5
= 26.0\ \text{kN}.

Required thrust at cruise: 22.12\ \text{kN}. Margin:

\frac{26.0 - 22.12}{22.12} = 17.5\%.

O.2 One module failure

Remaining continuous thrust:

F_{\text{remain,cont}} = 3 \times 6.5 = 19.5\ \text{kN}.

This is below the cruise requirement, so the capsule must decelerate. The maximum deceleration available without overloading is

a_{\text{decel}} = \frac{mg - F_{\text{remain,cont}}}{m}
= \frac{19{,}620 - 19{,}500}{2000}
= 0.06\ \text{m/s}^2.

This is very low. However, the eddy-current retarder and mechanical brake are available. Additionally, the remaining modules can be temporarily overloaded to 8.5\ \text{kN} for 10\ \text{s}:

F_{\text{remain,peak}} = 3 \times 8.5 = 25.5\ \text{kN}.

This exceeds the cruise requirement, allowing the capsule to maintain speed or decelerate gently while reaching the nearest landing. The N-1 criterion is therefore satisfied with appropriate speed reduction.

